WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.510
ANDY CHANG: Hi, everyone.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.910
So what we're going
to talk about today

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.360
is really the process of
moving from your secure,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.240
on-premise to hybrid
and then into cloud.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.900
I think, as you've
heard throughout Next,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.290
at Google, we're invested in
trying to reduce the obstacles,

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.940
the concerns that you
don't need to have

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.040
to accelerate and elevate
the way you do development

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.570
so that you can grow businesses,
grow your applications,

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.510
and be successful.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:55.910
Especially in security,
what we're talking about,

00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.330
as George pointed out, is cloud
should be the immune system

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.200
for you to protect you against
cyber attacks-- something you

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.519
don't have to actively
manage, something

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:07.290
that, in fact, is
there in the background

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.140
always defending you.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.090
Now, we recognize
that not all of you

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.440
are in the same space
in terms of moving

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.780
to the third generation cloud.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.320
Many of you have
on-premise facilities,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.550
have needs to have
workloads that

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.990
are both in and
on-premise situation

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.300
as well as in the cloud.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.430
And really, the key is
to for us to give you

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.810
the controls to allow you
to manage the trust the way

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:34.400
you need.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.980
As Neal has pointed out, beyond
just the underlying security

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.520
and trust built into our
core platform beneath Google,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.080
we need to continuously
provide you

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.420
the controls to access
those capabilities so you

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.410
can secure your workloads
the way you need them to be.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:55.290
So at the core of what we're
talking about at Google,

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.160
the security is really based
on four key principles--

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.010
trust and transparency,
control, audit compliance,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.020
and innovation.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.290
And what we mean by this is
it's not just sufficient for us

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.150
to have superior technology.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.610
Trust that you have
in us and what we do

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.240
is really built in being
transparent about things.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.180
And that's part of Neil's
talk earlier today,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.770
is one of the steps to get you
a sense of what really goes on

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:22.320
behind the scenes.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.480
In addition, for control from
enterprises and businesses,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.280
you need the ability to have
access to the capabilities

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.690
to configure and
run your business

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:38.380
and control the user service
data access levels as you need.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.070
For audit and compliance,
our commitment

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:43.450
is to make sure
that, in addition

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.566
to the certifications
that we have on our cloud,

00:02:45.566 --> 00:02:46.940
we're going to be
able to support

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.770
you to get the
certifications that you

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.560
need to run your businesses.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.990
And of course, we're
focused on innovation,

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.240
whether it's the machine
learning that Jeff talked

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.750
about, essentially the
superpower of having

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.050
cloud machine learning added
to your capabilities-- really,

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:06.400
the ability to have you focus on
your business, and then for us

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.540
to take care of the rest.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.020
So for today's talk, we're
going to focus, however,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.580
on folks who are not yet
in the generation 3 cloud.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.040
And the common questions
we get in security

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.800
really fall into
five main categories.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.650
How do you manage identity
and access control

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.990
so that the policies which
you already have today

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:29.980
can be reflected in the
cloud that you move to?

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.800
Network security-- how do you
create the isolation and trust

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.650
zones that you need to segregate
your users an information

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.350
as you might like?

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.800
Data protection-- how is
your data, your user's data,

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:46.000
your customer's data protected,
and how do you control that?

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.880
Threats and vulnerabilities--
how really access

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.650
to and breaches and
intrusions are handled so

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:55.880
that you are protected upfront.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.860
And ultimately, audit
logging-- the things

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.210
that allow you to know what's
going on in your systems

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:04.220
to know every action.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.690
So in order to do
this, we're going

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:09.210
to use an example to
walk you through a step

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.310
by step process of migrating
from an on-premise application

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.800
to a hybrid situation where part
the application runs in cloud,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.010
and then finally, to
a full GCP instance.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:22.500
Now, to do this,
we tried to pick

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.410
a small representative
example application.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.540
So we've created a
Memegen application

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.275
which is built on a LAMP stack.

00:04:29.275 --> 00:04:33.450
It has a MySQL database
and an Apache front end.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:39.380
And basically, it allows
you to start out on premise,

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.380
and it will move it
step by step forward.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.890
So let's go.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.150
So the first step,
as we talked about,

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:50.570
was taking your existing
identities and your policies,

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:53.560
and how does that
come with you in cloud

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.180
so you don't have to
change and do other things.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.800
So to help me talk
through that, I'm

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.460
going to invite
Ameet Jani, who's

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.479
the product manager
for Identity for Cloud,

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:03.145
to walk you through
identity federation.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.950
Thank you.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.900
AMEET JANI: Thank you, Andy.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.680
Your voice.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.740
So Andy and I were walking
through a potential scenario

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.970
here, and we thought it
would be kind of fun-- fun

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.930
being relative, last day of
a conference kind of fun,

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.140
I guess-- to walk through
an actual customer

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.970
type of example,
to really represent

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.220
what our customers go through.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.150
Andy and I have talked
to a lot of customers,

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:32.620
and we're hearing--
we thought it

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.830
would be fun to walk through
what that would look like.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:38.080
So one of things we thought
we should walk you through

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.680
is the typical
scenario of a customer.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.240
You already have an
identity provider.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.490
You already have a source
of truth for your identities

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:45.500
where you've defined
users and groups.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.791
And what you really want is
just to carry that with you

00:05:47.791 --> 00:05:48.680
into the cloud.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.013
And so what you're going to
see-- let's do exactly that.

00:05:51.013 --> 00:05:52.980
We're going to use an IDP.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.030
We can talk to It.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.960
Insert your name of
your identity provider

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.252
here, whether it's Active
Directory or anybody else.

00:05:58.252 --> 00:06:00.710
And we're going to federate
your users in groups to Google.

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:01.900
And then the next thing
that we're going to do

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:03.234
is show you how those now exist.

00:06:03.234 --> 00:06:03.983
They're federated.

00:06:03.983 --> 00:06:06.590
All you have to do is assign
permissions to your groups,

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.370
and everybody is there.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.040
And then lastly, I
think just to prove

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.660
that this is a federated
identity use case,

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.909
we're going to delete a user
in your identity provider

00:06:13.909 --> 00:06:16.272
and show you how
quickly that reflects.

00:06:16.272 --> 00:06:18.230
So just to highlight what
we talked about here,

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:20.940
you'll see these very
complicated art graphics here.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.960
You'll see the non-Google
identity provider.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.810
Your identity is
your source of truth.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:26.351
It gets federated
to Google, and then

00:06:26.351 --> 00:06:28.680
you just assign the
users that are there.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.440
So why don't we
jump into the demo?

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.190
Andy is going to
drive it for us here.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.898
You'll see this is
our identity provider.

00:06:33.898 --> 00:06:35.470
In this case, we used Ping.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.350
You can use any identity
provider that you like.

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:41.140
Most of them come with a
Google connector, as Ping does.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.370
And so the idea
is, then, that you

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.340
connect Ping to your
Google environment,

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:47.090
and your source of
truth is now connected.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:49.970
So here you'll see, in this
very simplified example,

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.710
we have users that
are assigned here.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.490
And each of these users are
already assigned to a group.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:58.080
And so Andy will show you
here that these groups exist.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.020
And I think the key
he wants to focus on

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.600
are the network administrator,
the network viewer,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.287
and the security administrator.

00:07:04.287 --> 00:07:06.620
These are the sample types
of groups that we've created.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:08.786
And the users that we've
talked about earlier, which

00:07:08.786 --> 00:07:12.980
were Bob, Amy, and
AJ, who are assigned

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.100
to each of these groups.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.620
And we'll see how that carries
over when you get into cloud.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.800
So then once you
federate it-- here's

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.800
an example of that group--
once those users and groups are

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.800
federated, you'll be able
to come to your Google

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:27.040
Console, which Andy
will do now, and you'll

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.934
be able to see both of those.

00:07:28.934 --> 00:07:30.850
Just to prove that it
has, in fact, federated,

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.302
when you make a change in
your identity provider,

00:07:33.302 --> 00:07:34.260
the users show up here.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.585
So here you'll see Bob, AJ, and
of course, Amy reflected here.

00:07:37.585 --> 00:07:39.460
And they're already
assigned to those groups,

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.668
because the groups have also
been federated with you.

00:07:41.668 --> 00:07:44.224
So as you click into
AJ, for example,

00:07:44.224 --> 00:07:45.640
you'll be able to
see he's already

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:50.354
a member of this group-- in this
case, the network viewer group.

00:07:50.354 --> 00:07:51.770
And the same will
be true for Bob,

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:55.880
and the same will be true for
Amy as well in this example.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.630
Just to quickly
touch on those rules,

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:00.880
the rules are network
viewer is obviously

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.300
a read-only kind of role.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:04.990
A network admin is somebody
who can create new network

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.160
and take networking
type functionality.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.060
And a security admin, our
hero of the story here,

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:13.210
is the one who can do security
actions but not necessarily

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:15.420
network actions.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.365
So then why don't we
jump into the IAM window?

00:08:17.365 --> 00:08:18.740
So those of you
who are familiar,

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.500
you come into the
cloud console, you'll

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.152
see Andy has already
created those--

00:08:22.152 --> 00:08:23.360
the groups are already there.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.240
They pre-populate
when you do a search

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:26.765
or when you create a new member.

00:08:26.765 --> 00:08:28.140
And he's assigned
them to groups.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.390
So here you can see
the roles are already

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:30.960
assigned-- compute
network admin,

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.700
compute network viewer, and of
course, compute security admin.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:38.370
So those are predefined roles
that already exist within IAM,

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.190
and you can just assign
them to the group.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:42.130
Note here we didn't
assign a single user

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:45.260
to a role that automatically
gets inherited when you

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.700
become a member of that group.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.790
So we've done a setup here.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:52.415
Why don't we actually live a day
in the life of-- let's pretend

00:08:52.415 --> 00:08:53.900
we're Amy.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.600
Amy was the security
administrator.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:56.430
So Amy comes in.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.219
She logs into her single
sign on system here.

00:08:59.219 --> 00:09:01.010
And then she should
get redirected directly

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:04.616
into the Google console,
the Google Cloud Console.

00:09:04.616 --> 00:09:05.115
Or not.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.744
Just as a show of hands, how
many of you are using Ping?

00:09:35.744 --> 00:09:36.660
Just out of curiosity.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:37.535
Trying to understand.

00:09:37.535 --> 00:09:38.110
OK.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:41.424
I'm guessing most of you
are Active Directory.

00:09:41.424 --> 00:09:42.340
That's what I figured.

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:44.940
Are there others of
note that we should--

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.250
you can scream them out.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.560
AUDIENCE: Google.

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.970
AMEET JANI: Google, OK.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:53.600
So here you see can
see Amy is logged in.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.804
You can see that she's
in fact logged in.

00:09:55.804 --> 00:09:57.470
Because she's a
security administrators,

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:59.980
she really has no business
going through common dashboards

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:01.750
and GAE screens and GCE screens.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.802
So we'll jump into the
Networking screen here.

00:10:04.802 --> 00:10:06.510
Remember, she's a
security administrator.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.750
So when we come to
Networks as the option,

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.850
you'll see she cannot,
in fact, create networks.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:12.390
She can't do firewalls.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:13.830
She can't do VPNs.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.131
She can't do routers
and things like that.

00:10:16.131 --> 00:10:17.880
But in fact, when she
goes into firewalls,

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.338
she should be able to create
a firewall-- she is a security

00:10:20.338 --> 00:10:22.350
administrator-- and
take the action there.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.266
So again, she's already
been assigned the role

00:10:24.266 --> 00:10:26.090
without having to
explicitly do that.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:28.210
If we were to do the same
for Bob, for example--

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:31.540
we can come in and
be Bob for a moment--

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:33.420
you can see, in this
case, that's me.

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:44.857
There we are, Bob.

00:10:44.857 --> 00:10:46.440
Again, Bob is a
network administrator,

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.231
so no need to be in
any common screen here.

00:10:48.231 --> 00:10:49.740
But if you jump
into the networking,

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:52.198
you'll find what you expect--
that Bob can, in fact, create

00:10:52.198 --> 00:10:56.430
networks, can create VPNs,
can create DNS load balancers.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.170
But when you take it to the
firewall rules, of course,

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:01.269
he's unable to do
anything there.

00:11:01.269 --> 00:11:03.060
And then we can do the
same for AJ as well.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:04.830
AJ, again, had a
viewer role, so we'd

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:08.770
have-- we'll let Andy you
jump into the-- here we go.

00:11:11.252 --> 00:11:13.210
So then we can jump into
the Networking itself.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:17.480
And you'll find, in fact,
only read only screens.

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:19.350
It can only take
viewership, but can't

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:21.970
do any actions
themselves, as expected.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:23.360
No rocket science here.

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:27.590
So I think that's the high level
overview of the identity sync.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:30.140
Today, most commercial
and almost all open

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.620
source identity providers
have a natural bridge

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.314
into Google Apps and Google,
so that should naturally work.

00:11:35.314 --> 00:11:36.730
But let's walk
through an example.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:40.110
We talked about concluding here
with showing True Federation.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:41.490
So why don't we delete AJ?

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.080
And then when you go into Admin,
Console, and do a refresh,

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.090
you'll find that AJ--
fingers crossed-- will

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:57.938
have disappeared from all this.

00:11:57.938 --> 00:11:58.820
There you go.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:11:59.890
AJ is no longer there.

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:01.190
So it's a live system.

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:02.470
It happens instantaneously.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.780
So you don't have to-- the
minute somebody decides

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:06.200
to go on to better
opportunities and are

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:07.090
no longer at your
company, you're

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:08.860
able to remove them from
your identity provider,

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:11.276
and they completely get shut
down from access to the Cloud

00:12:11.276 --> 00:12:12.560
Console.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.200
So that's the
high-level summary here.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.284
Andy, why don't I
transition it back to you?

00:12:18.284 --> 00:12:21.180
ANDY CHANG: Thanks.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:23.560
So lesson number
1 from migrating

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:27.980
from your on-premise situation
to either a hybrid or cloud

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:30.290
is that you, in fact, can
bring your identities.

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:32.010
You don't need to recreate them.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.250
You're able to federate and
use the existing privileges

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.050
and IEM to enforce them.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:40.910
So the next thing we're going to
talk about is the hybrid cloud.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.100
And so what do we mean
by the hybrid cloud?

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:46.110
So today, what we're
going to talk to you about

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:51.190
is first from the Memegen app
in the on-premise situation.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:54.320
And you probably can't see
it, but this is actually

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:55.990
the on-premise server.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:13:00.350
So if we go to that
on-premise server,

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:03.540
this is the Memegen
app for that server.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:06.600
And it's a little,
tiny on-premise server.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.220
It's live.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:11.620
The other thing we'll
talk about is the project

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:15.090
that we just showed you
the controls and IEM for.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:18.370
So it's the Memegen
Next 16 application.

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:20.700
And today, we have a front end.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.290
So we've split it
now so that we're

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:25.260
going to keep the
back end on the server

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:28.770
and have the front
end show up in cloud.

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:32.000
So this is the front end,
the instance PHP piece.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.799
And right now, though, if I
go to that one, that website,

00:13:35.799 --> 00:13:37.590
you notice it's not
connecting to anything.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:40.180
Because we actually
haven't created a VPN,

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.805
a trusted connection
between the two.

00:13:41.805 --> 00:13:42.930
So we're going to fix that.

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:57.590
So what we're going to do now
is leverage the networking

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.700
that we just talked
about before.

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.520
So I'm logged in as Kanji.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:10.970
And Kanji, if you
might remember,

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:12.835
is one of the other
roles that I've created.

00:14:12.835 --> 00:14:17.275
It has both network
permissions and also has

00:14:17.275 --> 00:14:18.150
security permissions.

00:14:33.191 --> 00:14:35.440
So as someone who has network
permissions and security

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:38.210
permissions, he's
able to basically go,

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.210
as we said before,
not do anything

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:44.460
with the main dashboard, but he
can create both networks, VPNs,

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:45.190
and routes.

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:47.650
So we're going to create a VPN.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:50.189
So currently, there's a VPN.

00:14:50.189 --> 00:14:52.230
We're going to connect
the front piece, which was

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:55.190
the instance, to the back end.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.360
And we're going to use the
subnetwork that's created,

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:09.750
leveraging the subnet
technology we have.

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:12.010
It's in the US central region.

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:13.920
And we create a
static IP address

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.420
so we can do the VPN
connection, so we'll use that.

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:20.640
Now, on the other side, we need
a similar static IP address,

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:24.316
which we've locked in for
the on premise example.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.650
It's IKEV2, and we need
to share its secret.

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:38.500
Now, I would not recommend
this shared secret,

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:40.910
because it's a
little bit simple.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:42.899
But for the purposes
of this demonstration,

00:15:42.899 --> 00:15:44.440
we're going to go
ahead and use that.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:52.630
So now we have to use the local
connections here, and then

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:55.900
connect to the subnet.

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:58.360
Now, for the sake
of this demo, we're

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.790
showing this through menus.

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:05.680
But in fact, you can
have run G Cloud in RSDK,

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:07.950
so the same commands
can be scripted.

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:11.910
Or if you're running purely
in the services mode,

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:14.710
we give you the APIs to
allow you to run this, too.

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:18.020
So at scale, instead of
in this simple example,

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.340
you would be doing
this with automation.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.934
So let's go ahead
and create this VPN.

00:16:22.934 --> 00:16:25.350
So now it's going to take a
little bit of time for the VPN

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:25.850
to load.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:29.990
So while we're doing this, we'll
talk about what's going on.

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:32.820
So as we said before, from
the on-premise server,

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:37.270
we have the MySQL database, and
we're connecting to a front end

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:38.150
instance.

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:40.910
Each of the VMs has its
own individual firewall,

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:43.400
so we had to set up the
details for the firewall.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:45.320
And we'll show that
in a little bit on.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:47.370
The web application
is on the front end.

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:49.490
Now, each of the
individual identities

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:51.930
has different control
over the settings--

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.695
the ability to either control
the VPN, the firewall,

00:16:54.695 --> 00:16:57.420
and the underlying pieces.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:00.230
So what we're taking
advantage of here

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:04.690
is really several things in
the features on our network.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:06.894
The ability to create
private IP spaces

00:17:06.894 --> 00:17:09.310
so that when you connect to
us, you're no longer traveling

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:10.185
on the open internet.

00:17:10.185 --> 00:17:12.329
You're traveling within
our private network,

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:14.339
and your traffic stays
on our private network

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:15.726
encrypted as it moves forward.

00:17:15.726 --> 00:17:17.809
In addition, we're taking
advantage of the ability

00:17:17.809 --> 00:17:21.119
to have subnetworks, the
ability to really lock in

00:17:21.119 --> 00:17:23.069
and segment your
trust the way you

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:27.329
need to based on how you want
to control the connections

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:29.770
and access between roles.

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:32.160
And finally, we're taking
advantage of the VPN.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:35.456
So you notice now that the
VPN connection is green.

00:17:35.456 --> 00:17:36.830
So we're going to
go back and see

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:39.381
if we can hit that same
node before and see

00:17:39.381 --> 00:17:40.130
if it's connected.

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:51.480
And so we are.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:54.700
We're now connected, and we're
on our way to full cloud.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.900
So basically, we have the
database, the MySQL database

00:17:57.900 --> 00:17:59.440
connected on the back end.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:03.050
We have the PHP front
end serving up traffic.

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:04.780
So one of the steps
to the way there,

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.940
using our network and our
[INAUDIBLE] capabilities

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:08.550
to allow us to get there.

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:11.712
So lesson number 2-- you can
create the zones of trust

00:18:11.712 --> 00:18:14.170
that you need by leveraging
the capabilities in the network

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:15.519
that we've talked about.

00:18:15.519 --> 00:18:17.060
In addition, one of
the opportunities

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:20.440
when you have the
front end now on GCP

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:22.120
is to also leverage
the load balancing

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:24.619
that you heard about yesterday
in the networking talk, which

00:18:24.619 --> 00:18:27.450
gives you both performance,
scaling, and also

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:31.161
an amount of DDOS
protection built in.

00:18:31.161 --> 00:18:32.910
So the next thing that
we're talking about

00:18:32.910 --> 00:18:34.730
is going the full GCP.

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:46.250
So going back again
to the underlying--

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:52.580
so going back again from a role
standpoint to an owner role,

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:55.120
I can take a look at
again the overall project.

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:05.180
And when we go look at
the overall project,

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:08.240
we have-- in addition to
the front end that we just

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:13.570
triggered, which is
this one, we also

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:18.140
have the ability to go back
and look at the full LAMP stack

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:18.889
version.

00:19:18.889 --> 00:19:21.180
So what we've done is set up
a full LAMP stack version.

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:22.745
We've migrated everything over.

00:19:22.745 --> 00:19:24.870
The other thing, if you
guys were paying attention,

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.190
when I showed you each
of the first two--

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:30.440
the on premise one and
also the hybrid one--

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:33.310
those were actually
only in HTTP.

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:36.310
So at Google, obviously,
we're big fans

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:39.730
and implement TLS and SSH.

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:45.750
So this is actually a full HTTPS
connection on our Google Cloud

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:50.990
platform implementing the MySQL
database as well as the PHP

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:52.940
front end.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:56.180
Now, once you get
fully on our cloud,

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:59.180
the question is you have
now moved your data here,

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:01.812
but how do we connect
and continue to connect

00:20:01.812 --> 00:20:03.020
so that you can do this fast.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:05.300
So we have a large number
of different ways for you

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:07.880
to migrate your data
onto our cloud--

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:09.930
through VPN, through
direct peering,

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:11.690
through internetworking.

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.900
In addition, once your
data is in our cloud,

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:17.150
really the question is
how do we protect it.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:19.280
So we really give
you three options

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:20.800
of how to protect your data.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.480
First, by default,
your data is encrypted.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:25.700
And it's actually
encrypted three ways.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:29.650
The underlying data is
first charted and chunked.

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:32.130
Then each of those
pieces are encrypted.

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:35.780
The chunk key is then
encrypted, and a wrapping key

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:37.660
around the chunk
key is encrypted.

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:40.650
And those are then stored in
a key store, which manages

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:43.210
and rotates them as necessary.

00:20:43.210 --> 00:20:46.419
Or if you want to encrypt your
data before you give it to us,

00:20:46.419 --> 00:20:47.710
you certainly have that option.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:50.570
You have full
control in that case.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:54.165
Finally, you can give
us customer supply keys,

00:20:54.165 --> 00:20:56.290
and then we can go ahead
and encrypt things for you

00:20:56.290 --> 00:20:58.456
without us being able to
actually look at your data.

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:03.260
Additionally, yesterday
I think we talked to you

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:04.230
about audit logging.

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:06.646
So what we're going to show
you is-- this is a screenshot.

00:21:06.646 --> 00:21:09.360
We're going to show you the live
logs, again, off of this GCP

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:09.860
instance.

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:28.440
So what we're seeing here
now is the events that

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:30.140
created after the firewall.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:33.550
We can look at the events that
created off the Meme network,

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:34.606
which a the subnetwork.

00:21:34.606 --> 00:21:36.480
And all of those things
are accessible to you

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.160
both in the activity stream.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:39.310
You can search on them.

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:41.980
We can filter out
purely on activity.

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:46.170
We can filter out on looking
at a range of resources

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:49.190
within all the resources
within compute.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:51.150
And if you look at all
the different resources,

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:55.760
the choices allow you
to look at a wide range

00:21:55.760 --> 00:22:03.170
of both packets, changes in IEM,
permissions, and other pieces.

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:06.160
So finally, when you think
about going onto a platform,

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:08.660
it's important that
the underlying platform

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:12.200
has the governance and
the compliance pieces

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:14.930
that you need in case you need
to run those for your business.

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:20.130
So the Google Cloud platform is
regularly certified for SOC 2

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:26.760
and SOC 3, ISO 27001,
and then PCI DSS 30.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:29.190
And we're engaged
in supporting you

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.300
to get the end-to-end
certifications as you

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:34.480
run your business partially
on our cloud as well

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:39.340
as the other pieces that you're
running in your own business.

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:42.710
So lesson number 3-- once you
are comfortable with bringing

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:45.480
the data onto our
cloud, you're then

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:48.540
fully encrypted by default,
and you're in TLS everywhere.

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:53.590
But this is really
just getting you

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:58.486
to where you are now moved
from on-premise to a cloud

00:22:58.486 --> 00:23:00.360
environment, but you're
still, at this point,

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:03.440
using the cloud in a
way similar to how you

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:05.230
have before in your on-premise.

00:23:05.230 --> 00:23:09.860
As we've talked about
throughout Next, what we really

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:11.770
want you to be
able to do is focus

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:14.070
on building great apps
and great businesses

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:16.460
and let us take care
of the operations

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:19.130
and really live in a
serverless environment.

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.124
So since we've been
talking about security,

00:23:21.124 --> 00:23:22.540
another thing you
may have noticed

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:26.160
is we talked about LAMP
stack, but it's all in PHP,

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:27.830
so how do you deal with that.

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:31.800
So PHP has a range of
security vulnerabilities

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:33.480
that people try to exploit.

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:37.330
And part of that, then, is for
us to implement, as we have,

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.180
automated scanning for you.

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:41.360
So if you bring your
applications to our App Engine

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:44.470
product, we have a general
availability cloud security

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:45.390
scanner.

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:48.360
It's a website
scanner that basically

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:51.880
finds some of the most common
vulnerabilities proactively

00:23:51.880 --> 00:23:54.590
before you deploy.

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:57.660
An example here is if you
look at the OWASP top 10--

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.780
injection, cross-site
scripting, components

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:03.020
with known vulnerabilities,
those are all very

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:06.870
common things that occur in
web apps and cause breaches.

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:10.130
So our security scanner
detects cross-site scripting,

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:12.520
mixed content,
insecure libraries.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:15.200
And in 2016, we'll be
rolling out in addition

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:17.240
to SQL injection
and also the ability

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:23.740
to scan applications on GCE
and other installations.

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.000
In addition, we'll be
able to tie user features

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.140
around notifications
for you and the ability

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:29.980
to automate those scans.

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:33.704
So now we're going to show
you a little bit actually

00:24:33.704 --> 00:24:34.620
how the scanner works.

00:24:55.890 --> 00:25:00.700
So what we have here
is an application,

00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:02.140
which we call Firing Range.

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:04.310
We open sourced that
a couple years ago.

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:09.720
It is the basis on which most
web vulnerability scanners test

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.790
against to make sure
they find sources

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:17.029
of cross-site scripting, mixed
content, and library pieces.

00:25:17.029 --> 00:25:18.570
So what we're going
to do is show you

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:20.730
what one of these scan
results look like.

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:22.700
So in this project,
we have an application

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:24.780
called Public Firing Range.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:27.620
If you're in App Engine, you
get a tab that's specifically

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:30.550
Security Scans, and
then you're able to look

00:25:30.550 --> 00:25:33.250
at a range of scan results.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:37.070
So if we look at the scans
that we ran last night,

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:40.530
it hit 657 URLs
in the application

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:44.170
and over 600 vulnerabilities.

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:48.680
And those vulnerabilities range
from cross-site scripting,

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:53.960
420 of them, and old
library, mixed content.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:56.420
So for example, the
particular application

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:59.240
uses jQuery that's out of date.

00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:01.240
So that would be an
immediate flag and something

00:26:01.240 --> 00:26:02.350
you could fix.

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:07.080
In addition, we have in detail
for the cross-site scripting

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:09.140
errors the ability
for you to drill down

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:11.470
into each of these
errors, and in fact, both

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:13.780
replicate it to find
out where it actually

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:16.490
occurs in your code,
fix it, remediate it.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.930
And this is all in
development before you

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:20.090
deploy to production.

00:26:20.090 --> 00:26:24.850
So it catches things before
they actually hit production.

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:28.310
Currently, what we've seen
with folks who have used

00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:31.400
the security
scanner is that when

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:34.380
it's a cross-site scripting
error, once identified,

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:38.450
80% of the users go back and
in fact fix it and remediate.

00:26:38.450 --> 00:26:41.025
So that's a large number of
potential vulnerabilities

00:26:41.025 --> 00:26:43.510
that have been eliminated
before any of the code hits

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:45.480
production.

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:47.560
So the lesson, really,
then is continue

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.060
to move to higher
levels of abstraction.

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:52.130
Abstraction and automation
are your friends.

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:55.060
And we provide you the
ability to leverage that today

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:58.550
through App Engine, through
BigQuery, and through Dataflow

00:26:58.550 --> 00:27:03.200
so that you're really living the
serverless, no operations mode.

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:04.830
And that becomes the
next step of what

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:07.415
you can do once you've
gone from on premise

00:27:07.415 --> 00:27:13.080
to hybrid to GC and then to the
true third generation cloud.

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:23.260
So finally, what's
next after this?

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:27.110
In recap, what we've talked
about is identity federation--

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:28.930
the ability for you,
when you already

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:34.830
have in place installs,
to take those with you,

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:36.670
for you not to have
to recreate them,

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:40.040
and for us to basically
respect them, enforce the roles

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:42.410
and access policies
that you had.

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:45.110
In addition, we talked
about the network security

00:27:45.110 --> 00:27:47.100
needed to isolate
and create the trust

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:51.575
zones that you have through
VPN, through the subnetworking.

00:27:51.575 --> 00:27:53.710
We talked about the
data protection,

00:27:53.710 --> 00:27:57.450
the built-in encryption
as well as the control

00:27:57.450 --> 00:28:00.460
that you have to further
encrypt and protect your data.

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:02.067
And then we talked
about the logging

00:28:02.067 --> 00:28:03.775
and then the abstractions
and automation.

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:09.480
So ultimately, what's
next after this?

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:11.690
From a cloud security
side, those of you

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:14.590
who have done some of the code
labs, the specific details

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:18.160
of how we built each of these
pieces, which we couldn't get

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:20.780
into today on stage,
you can actually

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:23.700
walk through each
step in the code lab.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:26.680
In addition, the cloud security
scanner highly recommend that

00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:29.600
you guys try it out--
its ability to find

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.100
vulnerabilities, its
ability to be very,

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:35.180
very low noise and high signal
so that you can remediate .

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:36.420
directly.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:39.412
And then on the app
side, once it's on GAE,

00:28:39.412 --> 00:28:40.870
there's a couple
different options.

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:43.160
We talked about going
to App Engine directly

00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:45.090
or to App Engine
Manage VMs, where

00:28:45.090 --> 00:28:47.440
you can bring your own
programming system that you're

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:49.570
more comfortable and
familiar with-- moving

00:28:49.570 --> 00:28:52.730
the MySQL to Cloud
SQL, and particularly

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:55.480
for this application,
which is image centric,

00:28:55.480 --> 00:29:00.310
to take advantage of the machine
vision APIs that we have.

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:04.260
So with that, again,
for us, the ability

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:06.820
to go for a third
generation cloud

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:09.800
is really the ability to
combine security and innovation

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:11.380
and build what's next.

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:13.894
So thank you guys,
and both Ameet

00:29:13.894 --> 00:29:16.060
and myself will be around
if you have any questions.

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:19.410
[APPLAUSE]

